  "downloadSentinel": "Liver Mass With Central or Eccentric Scar\n\nCore Validation Gate: PASSED — Pathway A. I retrieved and reviewed the relevant material from Core Radiology: A Visual Approach to Diagnostic Imaging, 2nd ed. The principal source basis is GI chapter, Liver section, especially GI pp. 98, 105–115, 119, and 121–122. Core explicitly covers the central-scar framework and the major entities used here: FNH, giant hemangioma, conventional and fibrolamellar HCC, hepatic adenoma, metastases, mass-forming cholangiocarcinoma, epithelioid hemangioendothelioma, and the Budd-Chiari/regenerative-nodule context.\n\nCore validation digest: Core identifies the classic central-scar triad as FNH, fibrolamellar HCC, and giant hemangioma; distinguishes FNH by homogeneous arterial enhancement, T2-bright scar, late extracellular scar enhancement, and hepatobiliary retention; distinguishes hemangioma by peripheral discontinuous blood-pool enhancement with progressive centripetal fill-in; distinguishes fibrolamellar HCC by a large heterogeneous mass with a fibrotic T1/T2-dark scar; and supplies complementary patterns for HCC, adenoma, metastases, cholangiocarcinoma, EHE, and regenerative nodules.\n\nCore Summary: The most useful organizing move is to decide whether the apparent “scar” is a true fibrous/vascular scar versus necrosis or degeneration, then use enhancement pattern, scar T2 signal, hepatobiliary uptake, calcification, multiplicity, capsular retraction, and vascular/biliary invasion to rank the differential. FNH behaves like functioning hepatocellular tissue and retains Eovist; hemangioma follows the blood pool; conventional HCC arterializes and washes out and preferentially invades veins; fibrolamellar HCC is heterogeneous with a fibrotic scar; cholangiocarcinoma enhances progressively because of fibrous stroma; EHE favors multiple peripheral coalescent target lesions with capsular retraction; adenoma is heterogeneous from fat/hemorrhage and lacks bile ducts; and metastases are commonly multiple and typically portal-venous-phase conspicuous.\n\nWhole-source coverage audit completed from full relevant Core coverage and the complete RadPrimer article provided in your prompt. Core Summary was derived from the Core material itself, not from the validation digest. Card generation used the full-source review, not the digest or summary alone.\n\nSECTION A case ledger: Total user-provided cases: 14. Eligible for PRIMARY UNKNOWN: 14. Excluded: 0. PRIMARY UNKNOWN cards generated: 14. SECTION A cardinality check passed: eligible case count matches PRIMARY UNKNOWN count. All 20 supplied images are accounted for in their designated 14 cases, with multi-image stacks preserved and exact captions retained.\n\nNo standalone Differential Drills or Definition cards were generated because neither was explicitly requested. The autonomous section audits produced 6 Mechanism cards, 2 Boards Trap cards, and 33 High-Yield cards, including Core-supported CT, MRI, ultrasound, nuclear-medicine, scar-discriminator, epidemiology, structural-framework, and report-critical pivots.\n\nFinal pre-TSV audit: Total cards to export: 55. Expected TSV lines: 55. Actual TSV lines: 55. Each line contains exactly 22 columns, the Tags column is blank, all card fields are single-line TSV-safe, UNKNOWN contexts contain unique 12-character codes, all image references are preserved, and the supplied captions passed the verbatim-integrity check.",
